      "math_academy_id": "q-46081",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "The work $W$ applied to a body is related to force $F$ and distance $d$ by the equation $W=F\\cdot d.$ If $F$ is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})$ and $d$ is measured in meters, what is an appropriate measurement unit for work?",
      "worked_solution": "To find the unit of work, we substitute the given units of force and distance into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =F\\cdot d \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$",
